        <div id="floor-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Know Your Floor: Hardwood, Engineered, Laminate or Vinyl</h2>
<p>
            Most people searching for a hardwood mat have one of four floors, and each fails in a
            different way.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Solid and engineered hardwood</h3>
          <p>
            Real wood is the most forgiving of mat materials but the most prone to dents. Softer
            species such as pine, and even oak under a narrow foot, can take a permanent impression
            from a heavy machine standing in one place for months. Wood also moves with humidity, so
            a mat that traps moisture can cause cupping or discolouration underneath. A wood floor
            can usually be refinished; a dent across several boards is still a nuisance to fix.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Laminate</h3>
          <p>
            Laminate has a hard, printed wear layer over a fibreboard core. The surface resists dents
            better than soft wood, but the core swells if water gets into the joints, and swollen
            laminate cannot be repaired, only replaced. Laminate floors also float, so a treadmill's
            vibration can make them click or flex. A mat that spreads the load and keeps sweat out
            of the seams matters more here than anywhere.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Luxury vinyl plank and tile</h3>
          <p>
            LVP and LVT are waterproof and fairly dent-resistant, but they are the floors most often
            covered by a warning about rubber. Several vinyl floor makers advise against rubber or
            latex-backed mats because a reaction between the rubber and the vinyl can leave a
            permanent yellow or brown stain. Check your floor maker's care guide; if it warns against
            rubber, use a PVC mat and lift it to check for marks after the first week.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Bamboo, cork and painted floors</h3>
          <p>
            Strand-woven bamboo behaves much like a hard wood. Cork is soft and dents easily, so
            spread the load with the firmest mat you can find. Painted or oiled boards can transfer
            colour to a mat or pick it up from one, so treat them like vinyl and check early.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="materials" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Rubber or PVC: Staining, Smell and Grip</h2>
<p>
            Almost every treadmill mat is one of three materials. On a hard floor, the differences
            matter more than on carpet.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>PVC and vinyl.</strong> Most mats sold as treadmill mats are PVC, from thin
            flexible sheets to denser heavy-duty mats. PVC grips the floor, wipes clean, does not
            absorb sweat and has little smell after airing. It is the safe default for laminate and
            LVP. Even so, any mat can mark a floor in the wrong conditions, so lift it after a week
            to check.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Recycled rubber.</strong> Made from ground tyres bound together, rubber is dense,
            heavy, very grippy and spreads a heavy machine's load well. It is the material gyms use.
            The drawbacks indoors are smell, which can last weeks in a closed room, black marks if the
            surface sheds, and the staining risk on vinyl described above. On real hardwood, the
            staining risk is lower, and a rubber mat's weight helps it stay flat; check your floor's
            care guidance all the same.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>EVA and other foams.</strong> Lightweight foam mats feel soft and are cheap, but
            foam compresses where the feet sit and can lose its spring. Under a heavy machine, the
            feet can sink until they press into the floor almost as if the mat were not there.
            Foam suits lighter treadmills and folding machines better than heavy non-folding ones.
          </p>
          <p>
            Our main guide to <a href="/best-treadmill-mats/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill mats</a> covers the same materials from the point of view of noise and machine size.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="dents" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Preventing Dents From the Feet</h2>
<p>
            A treadmill does not dent a floor because it is heavy; it dents a floor because its
            weight, plus yours as you land, is concentrated on a few small feet. The fix is to spread
            that load over a larger area.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Choose a dense mat over a soft one.</strong> A dense PVC or rubber mat spreads
            the load from each foot across the area around it. A soft foam mat compresses and passes
            more of the point load straight through.</li>
            <li><strong>Add pads under the feet on soft floors.</strong> On soft wood or cork, a wide,
            dense pad under each foot, on top of the mat, spreads the load further. Our guide to <a href="/best-treadmill-anti-vibration-pads/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill anti-vibration pads</a> covers rubber and cork pads that also cut vibration.</li>
            <li><strong>Level the machine.</strong> A treadmill that rocks puts more of its weight on
            two diagonal feet. Use the adjustable feet, if it has them, so all four bear evenly.</li>
            <li><strong>Watch the wheels.</strong> Many folding treadmills rest on transport wheels
            when folded, and some on rear wheels when in use. Narrow plastic wheels dent more than
            flat feet. Make sure the mat is under them in every position.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="moisture" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Moisture, Sweat and Trapped Grit</h2>
<p>
            The damage that a mat itself can cause on a hard floor usually comes from what gets
            trapped under it. Two things matter.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Grit.</strong> Dust and sand under a mat act like sandpaper as the treadmill
            vibrates. Sweep and wipe the floor before laying the mat, and lift it every month or two
            to clean underneath. If the mat moves at all during use, fix that first; a mat that shifts
            grinds grit into the finish.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Moisture.</strong> A mat larger than the machine catches sweat and drips that
            would otherwise run into floorboard gaps or laminate joints. Wipe the mat after sweaty
            sessions. In a humid basement or a room with a recently laid floor, lift the mat
            occasionally so the floor underneath can breathe, and dry anything that has got under it
            before putting it back. A drink spilled on a treadmill can reach the floor through the
            frame; our guide to <a href="/treadmill-water-damage/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill water damage</a> covers the machine side of that.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="smell" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The New-Mat Smell</h2>
<p>
            Most new mats smell of something when they come out of the box, and rubber smells
            strongest, and some sellers of cheaper PVC mats say on their listings that the mat should
            be left to air before use. In a garage it does not matter; in a living room or bedroom it
            can.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Unroll the mat outside, on a balcony or in a ventilated garage for a few days before
            bringing it in.</li>
            <li>Wash it with mild soap and water and let it dry fully; that removes some of the
            surface residue.</li>
            <li>Keep it out of direct sun while airing it, which can warp PVC.</li>
            <li>If a smell persists for weeks in a small room, a PVC mat is likely to be easier to
            live with than rubber.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="setup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Laying the Mat on a Hard Floor</h2>
<p>
            Lay the mat with the treadmill off the floor if you can, rather than dragging the machine
            onto it, which can ruck the mat and scratch the floor. With two people, tilt the machine,
            slide the mat under, and lower it. Then:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Leave a margin of mat on every side, and more at the back, where you step off.</li>
            <li>Check that the mat lies flat under every foot; a ridge under one foot makes the deck
            rock and stresses the frame.</li>
            <li>Keep the power cord off the mat's edge and well clear of the machine, so it cannot be
            pinched or tripped over.</li>
            <li>Leave clearance behind the treadmill. Our <a href="/treadmill-safety-tips/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill safety guide</a> covers how much.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you also want quieter running, a mat on a hard floor reduces impact noise through the
            structure more than it reduces motor noise. Our guide to <a href="/best-treadmills-for-apartments/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills for apartments</a> covers the rest.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who Should Buy Something Else</h2>
<p>
            <strong>If your treadmill is a walking pad</strong>, these mats are far bigger than you
            need; see our guide to <a href="/best-walking-pad-mats/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad mats</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If your treadmill shares the room with weights</strong>, consider gym flooring
            across the whole area instead of a single mat.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If the floor is already damaged</strong> or uneven, level the treadmill on its
            adjustable feet first; a mat does not fix an uneven base.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Protecting Hard Floors</h2>
          <p>Each of these leaves the floor marked even with a mat in place.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Putting a rubber mat on vinyl plank</h3>
              <p>Several vinyl floor makers warn that rubber can permanently discolour their floors. Check your floor's care guide before choosing a mat, and on vinyl or LVP use a PVC mat instead.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Buying a mat smaller than the machine</h3>
              <p>Feet or wheels left on the bare floor do the damage the mat was bought to prevent. Measure the treadmill's full footprint, deck down and folded, and buy a mat larger on every side.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Choosing soft foam for a heavy treadmill</h3>
              <p>Foam compresses under a heavy machine's feet and passes the load straight to the floor. Use dense PVC or rubber, and pads under the feet on soft woods.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Never cleaning under the mat</h3>
              <p>Grit and moisture trapped under a mat scratch and stain a hard floor. Sweep before laying it and lift it every month or two to clean and dry underneath.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">Match the mat to the floor before the machine.</strong> On laminate
            and vinyl plank, use a dense PVC mat such as ProsourceFit's or Dimex's, larger than the
            treadmill on every side; for a big machine, Sunny's Large mat covers the footprint.
          </p>
          <p>
            Save rubber, such as Rubber-Cal's mat, for real hardwood under heavy machines, and only once
            you have checked your floor's care guidance. Whatever you choose, clean under it every
            month or two. For more sizes and materials, see our guide to <a href="/best-treadmill-mats/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">treadmill mats</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

