        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Do you need a mat under a treadmill on hardwood floors?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes. A treadmill's feet concentrate its weight, and yours, on small areas that can dent wood, and vibration grinds any grit into the finish. A mat spreads the load, keeps grit and sweat off the floor and stops the machine creeping. It is cheap compared with refinishing boards.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Will a rubber treadmill mat damage hardwood or vinyl floors?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It can damage vinyl. Several vinyl and LVP floor makers warn that rubber can cause permanent discolouration, so use PVC on those floors. On real hardwood the risk is lower, but check your floor's care guidance and lift the mat after a week to look for marks.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the best treadmill mat for laminate floors?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              A dense PVC mat larger than the treadmill on every side. Laminate's surface resists dents fairly well, but water getting into the joints makes the core swell, so a wipe-clean mat that catches sweat and drips matters most. Keep it flat and clean under it every month or two.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How do I stop my treadmill denting a wood floor?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Spread the load. Use a dense mat rather than soft foam, add wide, dense pads under each foot on soft woods, level the machine so all feet bear evenly, and make sure the mat is under any wheels the machine rests on, folded or unfolded.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How do I get rid of the smell of a new treadmill mat?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Air it outside or in a ventilated garage for a few days, out of direct sun, then wash it with mild soap and water and let it dry. Rubber smells strongest and longest; in a small room, a PVC mat is easier to live with.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How thick should a treadmill mat be for hardwood?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Thickness matters less on a hard floor than density. A dense mat of 3 to 6 millimetres protects the finish well; on soft wood under a heavy machine, add pads under the feet rather than buying a thick, soft mat that compresses.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

